===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|payen}}, from {{der|en|fro|paiier||pay}}, from {{der|en|ML.|pācāre||to settle, satisfy}} from {{der|en|la|pācō|pācāre|to pacify}}. In this sense, displaced native {{ncog|ang|ġield||pay}} and {{m|ang|ġieldan||to pay}}, whence {{w|Modern English}} {{m|en|yield}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb|pays|paying|paid|past2=payed|past2_qual=obsolete}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[give]] [[money]] or other compensation to in exchange for goods or services.
#: {{ux|en|he '''paid''' him to clean the place up}}
#: {{ux|en|he '''paid''' her off the books and in kind where possible}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=17|passage=This time was most dreadful for Lilian. Thrown on her own resources and almost penniless, she maintained herself and '''paid''' the rent of a wretched room near the hospital by working as a charwoman, sempstress, anything.}}
#* {{quote-video game|en|developer=w:BioWare|title=w:Mass Effect 3|location=Redwood City|publisher=Electronic Arts|year=2012|system=PC|scene=Normandy SR-2|oclc=962368035|text=Admiral Hackett: You can '''pay''' a soldier to fire a gun. You can '''pay''' him to charge the enemy. But you can't '''pay''' him to believe.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-21|author=w:Oliver Burkeman
|volume=189|issue=2|page=48|magazine=w:The Guardian Weekly
|title=[http://www.theguardian.com/technology/2013/may/10/conscious-computing-twitter-facebook-google The tao of tech]
|passage=The dirty secret of the internet is that all this distraction and interruption is immensely profitable. Web companies like to boast about{{...}}and so on. But the real way to build a successful online business is to be better than your rivals at undermining people's control of their own attention. Partly, this is a result of how online advertising has traditionally worked: advertisers '''pay''' for clicks, and a click is a click, however it's obtained.}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To discharge, as a debt or other obligation, by giving or doing what is due or required.
#: {{ux|en|she offered to '''pay''' the bill}}
#: {{ux|en|he has '''paid''' his debt to society}}
#* {{RQ:KJV|Psalms|37|21|passage=The wicked borroweth, and '''payeth''' not again.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Lucrece|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=SjYOAQAAMAAJ&pg=PT38|passage=The petty ſtreames that '''paie''' a dailie [[debt|det]] / To their ſalt ſoveraigne with their freſh [[falls|fals]] [[haste|haſt]], / Adde to his flowe, but alter not his [[tast|taſt]].}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2013-06-22|volume=407|issue=8841|page=68|magazine=w:The Economist
|title=[http://www.economist.com/news/finance-and-economics/21579860-g8-pledges-tackle-three-ts-t-time T time]
|passage=Yet in “Through a Latte, Darkly”, a new study of how Starbucks has largely avoided '''paying''' tax in Britain, Edward Kleinbard [&hellip;] shows that current tax rules make it easy for all sorts of firms to generate what he calls “stateless income”: [&hellip;]. In Starbucks’s case, the firm has in effect turned the process of making an expensive cup of coffee into intellectual property.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To be profitable for.
#: {{ux|en|It didn't '''pay''' him to keep the store open any more.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[give]] (something else than money).
#: {{ux|en|to '''pay''' attention}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer|V|i|passage=not '''paying''' me a welcome}}
#* {{RQ:Marshall Squire's Daughter|A Court Ball|9|They stayed together during three dances, went out on to the terrace, explored wherever they were permitted to explore, '''paid''' two visits to the buffet, and enjoyed themselves much in the same way as if they had been school-children surreptitiously breaking loose from an assembly of grown-ups.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To be [[profitable]] or worth the effort.
#: {{ux|en|crime doesn’t '''pay'''}}
#: {{ux|en|it will '''pay''' to wait}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To discharge an obligation or debt.
#: {{ux|en|He was allowed to go as soon as he '''paid'''.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To suffer consequences.
#: {{ux|en|He '''paid''' for his fun in the sun with a terrible sunburn.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To admit that a joke, punchline, etc., was [[funny]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=Jon Byrell|title=Lairs, Urgers and Coat-Tuggers|publisher=Ironbark|location=Sydney|page=294|passage=Sutho took a pull at his Johnny Walker and Coke and laughed that trademark laugh of his and said: `Okay. I'll '''pay''' that all right.'}}

